119: ## 3. Actual extending lifts on the cap root lines
120: 
121: Use the actual minimal root source \(S_i'\), its deck \(D_i\), and
122: the line \(N_i\) established by the source-line diagrams of F41--F49.
123: Its generic Picard point is \(aG_i'\). The source translation by
124: \(+G_i'\) extends on that actual minimal regular source. The generic
125: chord lift of (WS.6) differs from an everywhere invertible line map
126: only by a whole-fibre divisor: on a smooth root this follows from
127: the single reduced fibre, and on a reduced III, IV, II or I2 root
128: it follows from the zero component degrees and the primitive integral
129: kernel of its component intersection matrix. Remove that integer
130: base power. Denote the resulting extending map by \(L_i\).
131: 
132: All translation lifts on a degree-zero elliptic line commute. This
133: has an explicit generic-fibre model: write that line as
134: \((\mathbb C\times\mathbb C^*)/\Lambda\), with lattice action
135: \((z,v)\mapsto(z+\omega,\chi(\omega)v)\). The lifts are translations
136: in the first coordinate and scalar multiplications in the second,
137: which commute. Equality on the generic fibres extends over the actual
138: root line. In particular \(L_i\) commutes with the translation lift
139: \(F_i\) used in the cap's \(A_{i,0}\).
140: 
141: The deck commutator is a base unit:
142: \[
143:                       D_iL_iD_i^{-1}=\chi_i(z)L_i.
144: \]
145: Its cyclic norm is one. Divide by its constant value at zero, take
146: the logarithm \(g\) vanishing at zero, and put
147: \(\mathcal D f(z)=f(\zeta_{m_i}^{-1}z)\). The norm identity
148: gives \(\sum_j\mathcal D^jg=0\). The explicit function
149: \[
150:  h=-\frac1{m_i}\sum_{j=0}^{m_i-1}j\mathcal D^jg
151:  \quad\text{satisfies}\quad (\mathcal D-I)h=-g.
152: \]
153: Rescaling \(L_i\) by the target scalar \(e^h\) therefore makes
154: the commutator the constant
155: \(\chi_i=\chi_i(0)\). Once \(\chi_i^b=1\), the unit
156: \(L_i^b/B_0^{-1}\) is deck invariant. An invariant analytic b-th
157: root rescales \(L_i\) further so that
158: \[
159:  L_i^b=B_0^{-1},\qquad
160:  D_iL_iD_i^{-1}=\chi_i L_i,
161:  \qquad L_iA_{i,0}L_i^{-1}=\chi_i^{-1}A_{i,0}.          \tag{WS.9}
162: \]
163: The last equality uses the actual form \(A_{i,0}=\text{unit}\cdot
164: F_i^{n_i}D_i\) and the just-proved commutativity, including its
165: retained finite line character.
166: 
167: ### The good-root characters
168: 
169: On a good root let \(\rho_i\) be the deck derivative at a marked
170: fixed point of the central elliptic curve. The natural line
171: \(N_i=\mathcal O(aG_i'-O)\) has fibre weights \(\rho_i\) at
172: \(aG_i'\), \(\rho_i^{-1}\) at \(O\), and one at every other
173: fixed point. This is precisely the Cartier support-function line
174: map in F41/F42, including its exceptional correction, so it is
175: the actual cap line.
176: 
177: Translation by \(G_i'\) cycles the marked fixed points. Comparing
178: the fibre weights at two successive points gives its deck
179: commutator. For a good III or III* root the marked point has order
180: two, \(a\) is odd, and \(\rho_i\) has order four. The two successive
181: ratios are both \(\rho_i^2=-1\). For a good IV or IV* root the
182: marked point has order three. The three successive ratios are
183: all \(\rho_i^{-a}\). Thus
184: \[
185:  \chi_{\mathrm{III},\mathrm{III}^*}=-1,\qquad
186:  \chi_{\mathrm{IV},\mathrm{IV}^*}=\rho_i^{-a}.           \tag{WS.10}
187: \]
188: The actual minimal-root substitutions give
189: \(\rho_{\mathrm{IV}^*}=\zeta_3^2\) and
190: \(\rho_{\mathrm{IV}}=\zeta_3\). Consequently the single-cap
191: weighted E6 row \((a,b)=(2,3)\) and the ordinary IV row
192: \((a,b)=(10,3)\) both have \(\chi=\zeta_3^2\).
193: In the original E6 two-cap row \((1,6)\), the IV* character is
194: \(\zeta_3\) and the III character is \(-1\).
195: 
196: ### Reduced partners with the component class killed by a
197: 
198: In the weighted two-cap rows, a reduced cap is the partner on
199: which \(aG_i'\) has zero component class. Its central line is
200: trivial. One can check this on the actual subsequent good root:
201: after multiplication by \(a\), the marked section has reached
202: the identity point in the original minimal good model. Its
203: formal parameter vanishes to positive order. Substitution of a
204: root order \(m\ge2\) multiplies that order by \(m\). A nonzero
205: point in the additive identity group of a reduced II, III or IV
206: fibre has order exactly one in this good-root formal parameter,
207: as follows from \(x=w^2X,y=w^3Y\) and
208: \(-X/Y=w(-x/y)\). Therefore the root section specializes to
209: the zero element of the additive group. For the order-one root
210: the deck itself is the identity. In every case the central line
211: is trivial, or the deck is identity. A nonvanishing global
212: section of a trivial line on the connected proper central fibre
213: has a single deck eigencharacter, so comparison after translation
214: gives
215: \[
216:                               \chi_i=1.                 \tag{WS.11}
217: \]
218: This covers the reduced III partner of the E6 \((2,3)\) row,
219: the reduced IV partner of its \((3,2)\) row, the reduced II
220: partner of the E7 \((1,2)\) row, and the reduced IV partner
221: of the starred \((6,2)\) row.
222: 
